course: english
lesson: 33
title: Commas: lists, clauses and clarity
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A comma is a tiny signpost that tells the reader where one part of a sentence ends and the next begins, so the words are grouped the way you meant.
explain: The famous joke shows its power: "Let's eat, Grandma!" invites Grandma to dinner, while "Let's eat Grandma!" puts her on the menu; one little mark saves a whole gran.
explain: Commas separate items in a list ("apples, bananas and grapes"), follow an opening phrase ("After school, we went to the park"), and wrap extra information in a pair, like two hands holding it: "Sam, my friend, came round."
explain: But a comma is not glue: "It was raining, we went inside" sticks two whole sentences together with only a comma, which is called a comma splice; use a full stop, a joining word such as "so", or a semicolon.
explain: Commas are not sprinkles either: "The, dog, ran, quickly" is just noise; and in texts people use them loosely ("omg, wait, lol, ok"), which is fine for texting, while school writing follows the rules.
fact: Commas separate the items in a list: "I bought apples, bananas and grapes."
fact: A comma before the final "and" in a list is called the Oxford comma; most British schools leave it out, but it is not wrong and it can stop confusion.
fact: A comma goes before or after the name of the person you are talking to: "Let's eat, Grandma!"
fact: A comma usually follows a fronted adverbial, an opening phrase that tells when, where or how: "After school, we went to the park."
fact: A comma follows a subordinate clause that comes first: "When it rained, we went inside."
fact: Extra information in the middle of a sentence is wrapped in a pair of commas: "My sister, who is 12, likes horses."
fact: Joining two complete sentences with only a comma is a comma splice; use a full stop, a semicolon, or a joining word like "so" or "but".
fact: Before "however" joining two sentences, use a full stop or a semicolon, with a comma after it: "It rained; however, we played."
fact: Commas do not go between every word; too many commas make writing harder to read.
fact: Yes and no at the start of an answer are followed by a comma: "Yes, I can come."
fact: A comma also cuts off a little tag word added at the end, like "eh" or "innit": "It's cold, eh."
fact: Big numbers often use commas to group digits in threes: 1,000 and 25,000.
fact: Liverpool and Manchester speakers say "our kid" for a brother or sister and "me mam" for my mum; in formal writing these become "my brother" and "my mum".
exercise: Lily writes "I bought apples, bananas, and grapes." Her friend says the last comma is wrong. Who is right? | Neither: it is the Oxford comma, a style choice | Her friend, because a comma can never go near "and" | Lily, because every list must have a comma before "and" | Her friend, because lists use semicolons instead
exercise: Which sentence invites Grandma to eat? | Let's eat, Grandma! | Let's eat Grandma! | Lets eat Grandma! | Let's, eat Grandma!
exercise: Add the comma: "After school we went to the park." Where does it go? | After "school" | After "we" | After "went" | Between "the" and "park"
exercise: Jake from Liverpool says "Our kid, me mam and me went to town." Are the commas right? | Yes, they separate the list of people | No, lists of people need no commas | No, there should be a comma after every word | Yes, but only because "our kid" is slang
exercise: How would Jake write "our kid, me mam and me" in a formal report? | My brother, my mum and I | Our kid, me mam and myself | Me brother, me mum and me | My kid, my mam and me
exercise: Ella writes "My sister who is 12 likes horses." She has one sister. How should it be punctuated? | My sister, who is 12, likes horses. | My sister who is 12, likes horses. | My, sister who is 12 likes horses. | My sister who, is 12, likes horses.
exercise: Year 6: insert a comma to make sense of "Before eating the cat washed its paws." | Before eating, the cat washed its paws. | Before, eating the cat washed its paws. | Before eating the cat, washed its paws. | Before eating the cat washed, its paws.
exercise: Ahmed writes "The, dog, ran, quickly." What is wrong? | Commas don't go between every word | It needs more commas to be clear | It needs a comma before "The" | Dogs can't run quickly in a sentence
exercise: Mia, Gen Alpha, texts "omg, wait, lol, ok." Is she breaking the rules? | No, texting is informal and uses commas loosely | Yes, and she will lose marks in her next English test | Yes, because "lol" always needs inverted commas | No, because commas are banned in texts
exercise: Which sentence uses commas correctly? | Sam, my friend, came. | Sam my friend, came. | Sam, my friend came. | Sam, my, friend, came.
exercise: Zara writes "It was raining, we went inside." What is this mistake called? | A comma splice | A fronted adverbial | An Oxford comma | A run-on full stop
exercise: Which fixes the comma splice? | It was raining, so we went inside. | It was raining, and, we went inside. | It was, raining we went inside. | It was raining we went inside.
exercise: Max writes "It rained, however we played." Which is correct? | It rained; however, we played. | It rained however, we played. | It, rained however we played. | It rained, however, we, played.
exercise: Where does the comma go in "When it rained we went inside"? | After "rained" | After "When" | After "we" | After "inside"
exercise: Your nan asks "Coming for tea?" Which reply is punctuated correctly? | Yes, I'm coming. | Yes I'm, coming. | Yes I'm coming, | Yes, I'm, coming.
exercise: Which number is written with the comma in the right place? | 25,000 | 250,00 | 2,5000 | 25000,
test: Your mate goes "commas are just where you take a breath, innit." What is truer? | They group words to show meaning, not just breaths | They are only there for breathing when you read aloud | They mean you should stop reading for a second | They are decoration and change nothing
test: ★★★ "My brother who lives in Leeds" versus "My brother, who lives in Leeds". Which one suggests the speaker has more than one brother? | The one without commas | The one with commas | Both suggest exactly that | Neither says anything about it
test: A Gen Z sister texts "slay, babe, slay." Which comma rule is she using? | Commas before or after the person spoken to | Commas in a list of three foods | Commas after a fronted adverbial at the start | Commas around numbers
test: In a cartoon, a sign says "Slow children crossing." What comma would stop the joke? | Slow, children crossing | Slow children, crossing | Slow children crossing, | No comma can change it
test: What would happen if Ahmed took every comma out of "Before eating, the cat washed its paws"? | It would sound like someone ate the cat | Nothing, it means exactly the same thing | It would become a question | It would become a comma splice
test: A Canadian kid writes "I want pancakes, maple syrup, eh." ("Eh" is a friendly tag on the end.) Is the comma before "eh" fine? | Yes, it separates the tag at the end | No, "eh" is never written down | No, "eh" needs a full stop before it | Yes, because "eh" is part of the list
test: ★★★ "I love cooking my family and my dog." Which version clearly shows three separate things the speaker loves? | I love cooking, my family, and my dog. | I love cooking my family, and my dog. | I love, cooking my family and my dog. | I love cooking my family and, my dog.
test: Kai, Year 5, writes "Before the match, we had pizza, it was nice." What is wrong? | The second comma joins two sentences: a splice | The first comma, after "match", should not be there at all | "Pizza" needs capital letters | It needs a comma after "had"
test: Dad, bit grumpy: "Read it again, I said commas, not confetti." What is he telling his son? | Use commas only where a rule needs them | Use far more commas in every sentence he writes | Throw away all his commas | Commas are for parties
test: Which sentence uses the pair of commas to wrap extra information? | Mr Lee, our teacher, is tall. | Mr Lee our, teacher is tall. | Mr Lee, our teacher is tall. | Mr, Lee our teacher is tall.
test: ★★★ "We invited the clowns, Anna and Bea." With the Oxford comma "the clowns, Anna, and Bea" what changes? | It shows the clowns, Anna and Bea are separate guests | Nothing changes at all in meaning | It shows Anna and Bea are the clowns | It means Anna and Bea did the inviting
exam: Identify the sentence in which the comma serves to indicate the person being addressed. | Let's eat, Grandma! | Let's eat Grandma! | Let's, eat Grandma! | Let's eat Grandma, now!
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a comma splice? | Two complete sentences joined solely by a comma | A list of items separated by commas | A comma placed after an opening phrase | A pair of commas enclosing extra information
exam: Select the response consistent with established convention in respect of a fronted adverbial. | Later that day, we went home. | Later, that day we went home. | Later that day we, went home. | Later that, day we went home.
exam: Ascertain the correctly punctuated version of a sentence containing a non-essential relative clause. | My uncle, who lives in Cork, sings. | My uncle who lives in Cork, sings. | My uncle, who lives in Cork sings. | My, uncle who lives in Cork sings.
